# <%= current_page.data.title %>

## Overview

Container Platform 3 (CP3) provides full-text log search through a shared
[Amazon OpenSearch Service](https://aws.amazon.com/opensearch-service/) domain.
Each environment has one shared managed cluster — one for non-live, one for live
— used by every business unit: each unit's logs live in their own set of indexes,
and fine-grained access control keeps a unit's view to its own logs. You search
and build visualisations through OpenSearch Dashboards,
reached from an AWS-hosted OpenSearch user interface (OpenSearch UI) that you
sign into with your MoJ identity through IAM Identity Center — the same single
sign-on as the AWS access portal and Grafana.

> This page is a developer and platform-engineer guide to how log search works
> and how to use it. The Terraform that implements it lives in
> [modernisation-platform-environments](https://github.com/ministryofjustice/modernisation-platform-environments)
> under `terraform/environments/cloud-platform` (the `root` component,
> `opensearch.tf`), and the design is recorded in
> [ADR-017 Logging and Log Management Architecture](https://github.com/ministryofjustice/cloud-platform/blob/main/architecture-decision-record/cp30/ADR-017-logging-and-log-management.md)
> (see its OpenSearch section).

> **Status:** this is a development proof of concept. The cluster runs in the
> cloud-platform development account only, on a public (internet-facing)
> endpoint locked down by access control and HTTPS. A move to a private,
> in-VPC endpoint is planned once VPN access exists. Log ingestion from clusters
> is still being rolled out, so not every unit's logs are flowing yet.

## How it works

Each environment's shared, managed OpenSearch domain holds that environment's
logs. Business units are separated **inside** the cluster rather than by
separate clusters per unit:

- **Per-unit indexes** — each unit's logs go to indexes named
  `<business-unit>-<environment>-*` (for example `hmpps-non-live-*`).
- **Fine-grained access control (FGAC)** — a unit's role is scoped to its own
  index pattern, so a user sees only their own logs.
- **Encryption and HTTPS** — encryption at rest and in transit, TLS 1.3, audit
  logging to CloudWatch.

The domain runs OpenSearch 3.7, right-sized for the proof of concept. Why one
shared cluster rather than one per unit (cost, operational overhead, and the
isolation model) is set out in ADR-017.

## Signing in

Access is through the **OpenSearch UI application**, not the domain's own
Dashboards address (that has no interactive sign-in).

1. Open the OpenSearch UI application URL (published by the platform team).
2. You are redirected to IAM Identity Center; sign in with your MoJ identity if
   you are not already signed in.
3. You land in OpenSearch Dashboards.

Your access follows your **Identity Center group membership** — being in the
right group is enough, there is nothing to request per person. Your group does
need to be added to the application by the platform team first; once it is,
everyone in that group can sign in.

## Access model

| Role | Who | What you can do |
|---|---|---|
| Read-only | Every business unit, by default | Search your unit's logs; build and read visualisations and dashboards. |
| Elevated | Units that opt in | Read-only, plus safe self-service such as exporting search results. |
| Admin | Platform team | Full management of the cluster. |

Two things follow:

- You see **only your own unit's logs** — your role is scoped to your unit's
  `<business-unit>-<environment>-*` indexes.
- The elevated role is **opt-in per unit**, and each unit maps its own teams to
  it, so onboarding an engineer needs no platform-team change.

The platform team defines and provisions the roles and manages the cluster;
business units map their own teams and own index-level access within their own
indexes.

## Finding your logs

Create an index pattern for your unit — `<business-unit>-<environment>-*` — then
use Discover to search and the visualisation tools to build charts and
dashboards.

## Programmatic access

For scripts and tools, call the domain's REST API directly with an AWS-signed
(SigV4) request using your `platform-engineer` credentials, rather than the
browser UI. Browser sign-in is for interactive use only.

## Troubleshooting

- **"You don't have authorization to access dashboards"** — your Identity Center
  group has not been added to the application yet. Contact the platform team with
  your group name so they can add it.
- **Signed in but no indexes** — logs may not be flowing for your unit yet, or
  your index pattern does not match `<business-unit>-<environment>-*`.
- **Cannot reach the URL** — use the OpenSearch UI application URL, not the
  cluster's own Dashboards address.

